%erg 0.1
Title: roar and molt must purge review-pr panel scratch
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T16:02Z claude created
2026-09-16T16:05Z claude note Filed at the author's request after a /molt on git-erg left five dead worktrees standing, every one of them held open by a `.panel/<pr>/` directory and nothing else. Measured, not inferred: worktree-gc.sh reported `skip ... (uncommitted WIP)` for agent-a2f9efde52baf24ea, -a6a0158170957bafb, -a7f8ddde10e210fca, -aca79be11b484871c and -aea3969b25731b34b, and a directory listing of each showed a single `.panel/NNN/` entry (PRs 363, 346, 353, 347, 352 and 358) with no other untracked or modified file. The branches behind three of them were already merged and deleted; the trees are pure residue.

--- body ---
## Context

`/review-pr` writes its per-seat reports into `.panel/<pr>/` inside the worktree
it reviews, and `/gaze` does the same. Nothing removes them. They are untracked,
so every downstream gate that asks "is this tree clean?" answers no, forever.

Two gates are affected, and the author's request names the first:

1. **`/roar` step 9a**, `~/.claude/scripts/worktree-exit-preflight.sh`. It
   refuses to let a session exit its own worktree while untracked files are
   present. A session that ran `/review-pr` on its own PR therefore cannot
   dispose of its worktree at wrap-up, which is precisely when it should.

2. **`/molt` step 1.5**, `~/.claude/scripts/worktree-gc.sh`. It skips any
   worktree with uncommitted changes. Dead trees whose branches are merged and
   pruned accumulate indefinitely; on git-erg five had piled up by 2026-09-16,
   and the first `/molt` to look at them collected none.

The residue was noticed and explicitly left alone once before: the executor of
git-erg ticket 0292 hit `make test-encoding` tripping on a U+FFFD inside the
archived ticket copies `/review-pr` had staged under `.panel/`, moved the
directory out of the tree by hand, and wrote: *"I moved them out rather than
gitignoring, since that is a harness decision, not this ticket's."* This ticket
is that decision.

## The distinction the fix must preserve

Do not widen either gate to "ignore untracked files". The skip is load-bearing:
`/molt` step 1.5 and `/lair` step 8 both say in as many words that a worktree
skipped for uncommitted changes is a *signal*, because a closed ticket's dropped
deliverable looks exactly like this from the outside (harness ticket 0609, whose
mandated adherence test sat uncommitted for weeks after PR #1111 autoclosed the
ticket on the data fix alone).

So the fix names the scratch and purges only that:

- `.panel/` at the worktree root, and
- `build/panel-head/`, the checkout `/review-pr` builds to diff against.

Anything else untracked still blocks, still gets looked at, still gets reported.
A gate that stops distinguishing residue from a dropped deliverable has traded a
nuisance for a data-loss channel.

## Actions

1. Have the producer clean up after itself: `/review-pr` and `/gaze` remove
   their own `.panel/<pr>/` and `build/panel-head/` when they finish. This is
   the primary fix -- a gate purging another command's litter is the fallback,
   not the design.
2. `worktree-exit-preflight.sh`: treat the two paths above as scratch. Remove
   them, then re-evaluate cleanliness, and refuse as before on anything else.
3. `worktree-gc.sh`: same, before its uncommitted-changes test, so a tree held
   open by nothing but scratch becomes collectable.
4. Decide and record whether `.panel/` should also be gitignored in adopter
   repos. Note that this does NOT substitute for 2 and 3: an ignored file still
   exists on disk, and `git status --porcelain` with default flags would stop
   reporting it, which changes what both gates see. State the interaction rather
   than leave the next reader to rediscover it.

## Test

Red step, per case, before any fix:

- Build a worktree on a merged-and-deleted branch, put a `.panel/999/report.md`
  in it, run `worktree-gc.sh`, watch it print `skip ... (uncommitted WIP)`.
- In the same tree, run `worktree-exit-preflight.sh` and watch it exit 1.

Then the two positive controls, which matter more than the red step:

- The same tree with an untracked file that is NOT scratch -- say
  `tests/test_dropped.sh` -- must STILL be skipped and must STILL fail
  preflight, after the fix. This is the arm that proves the widening did not
  happen. Build it from git-erg 0292's real shape: a test file that a merged PR
  never committed.
- A tree with both a `.panel/` and a real untracked file must be skipped, and
  the report must name the real file, not the scratch.

Prove non-vacuity the per-case way: revert the scratch-purge hunk and watch the
scratch arm redden alone; hardcode the purge to match everything and watch the
dropped-deliverable control redden alone.

## Verification

- [ ] `/review-pr` and `/gaze` leave no `.panel/` or `build/panel-head/` behind
- [ ] a worktree held open by scratch alone is collected by `worktree-gc.sh`
- [ ] a worktree holding any non-scratch untracked file is still skipped, and
      the skip names that file
- [ ] `worktree-exit-preflight.sh` passes on scratch, refuses on anything else
- [ ] the gitignore question is answered in writing, with its interaction with
      the two gates stated
- [ ] the five git-erg worktrees standing on 2026-09-16 are collectable

## Invariants

- No gate stops distinguishing scratch from a dropped deliverable.
- Nothing is deleted outside the two named paths.
- `worktree-gc.sh` keeps its existing rails untouched: never a live process's
  cwd, never a locked tree, never the tree it runs from.

## Exit criteria

A session that reviewed its own PR can dispose of its worktree at wrap-up, and
`/molt` collects trees that nothing but review scratch is holding open -- while
a tree holding real uncommitted work is still stopped and still surfaced.

## Related

- 0928 -- the same panel, a different defect (it collapses to one sequential
  pass). No overlap in the fix.
- 0609 -- the dropped-deliverable incident the positive control is modelled on.
